"""Week45 Days 3-4: model complexity and deliberate feature representation."""

DEMAND_DEV = '''from lab import make_data
from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split
X, y = make_data(n=160, seed=145)
Xt, Xv, yt, yv = train_test_split(X, y, test_size=0.25, random_state=45)
'''

L(2, '1. Represent a curve with PolynomialFeatures',
  'Explain how regression can be linear in coefficients while curved in its inputs.',
  [('PolynomialFeatures; degree; include_bias', 'PolynomialFeatures creates powers and interaction columns. degree=2 includes squared and pairwise products. include_bias=False omits an extra constant column when the final estimator already fits an intercept.'),
   ('Linear in parameters', 'An equation b0+b1*x+b2*x² is linear in its fitted coefficients even though its prediction curve is not a straight line in x.')],
  'A straight line cannot represent every relationship. You can provide x and x squared as two feature columns, then fit an ordinary linear combination of those columns. The resulting curve changes with x, while the fitted coefficients still enter the equation linearly. PolynomialFeatures builds this representation consistently for training and future rows.\n\nKeep the feature transformer in a pipeline so prediction receives the same representation. With several inputs, polynomial expansion also creates interactions, and the number of columns can grow rapidly. A degree is a chosen model setting, not a fact learned automatically from the target.\n\nThe example uses an exact one-dimensional quadratic teaching relationship. It illustrates the representation and feature names, not an honest final model evaluation. Exact reconstruction on three fitted points does not show how a flexible polynomial will behave on noisy unseen observations. Part 2 adds that problem explicitly.',
  'import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import PolynomialFeatures\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression\nfrom sklearn.pipeline import make_pipeline\nX = np.array([[0.0], [1.0], [2.0]])\ny = 1 + 2 * X[:, 0] + 3 * X[:, 0] ** 2\np = PolynomialFeatures(degree=2, include_bias=False)\nprint("Columns:", p.fit(X).get_feature_names_out(["x"]).tolist())\nprint("Represented rows:", p.transform(X).tolist())\nm = make_pipeline(PolynomialFeatures(degree=2, include_bias=False), LinearRegression()).fit(X, y)\nprint("Prediction at1.5:", round(float(m.predict([[1.5]])[0]), 3))',
  'Expand two inputs guests/hours to degree two and list the resulting columns.',
  'from sklearn.preprocessing import PolynomialFeatures\np = PolynomialFeatures(degree=2, include_bias=False).fit([[10.0, 2.0], [20.0, 3.0]])\nprint(p.get_feature_names_out(["guests", "hours"]).tolist())',
  'Compare represented column counts for four original inputs at degrees one, two and three.',
  'from sklearn.preprocessing import PolynomialFeatures\nfor degree in [1, 2, 3]:\n    p = PolynomialFeatures(degree=degree, include_bias=False).fit([[1, 2, 3, 4]])\n    print("degree", degree, "columns", p.n_output_features_)',
  [('Is b0+b1*x+b2*x² linear in its fitted coefficients?', 'Yes', 'No because x is squared', 'Only when b2 is zero', 'Linearity in parameters refers to how fitted coefficients enter the equation.'),
   ('Why use include_bias=False with an intercept-fitting regressor?', 'Avoid an unnecessary duplicate constant feature', 'Remove every squared feature', 'Force the target to zero', 'The final estimator already provides the constant intercept term.')],
  'Assume increasing the polynomial degree automatically discovers the correct real relationship.', 'Treat the representation as a development choice and evaluate unseen cases.',
  'The transformer creates x and x²; the linear estimator fits coefficients for those columns. At 1.5 the known constructed formula gives 10.75. Its training fit is a representation demonstration, not a generalisation claim.')

L(2, '2. See polynomial underfitting and overfitting',
  'Compare polynomial degrees using held-out development error rather than training fit.',
  [('Model capacity; polynomial overfitting', 'Higher-degree representations can fit more detailed patterns, including noise. More capacity is not automatically better unseen performance.'),
   ('Development-only synthetic example', 'These noisy curves use a separate local simulation and validation grid. They do not open the final event-demand holdout.')],
  'Generate a small set of noisy observations around a smooth teaching curve. A straight line can miss its curvature. A very flexible polynomial can follow training noise and oscillate between observations. The question is how well a declared representation works on held-out development cases, not how closely it traces every training dot.\n\nThe plotted validation curve is known only because this is a simulation. A real project cannot consult an exact hidden truth curve. Here it makes the contrast visible and gives a controlled way to compute error. The printed training and validation MAE values are the evidence; do not infer a universal ranking from the plot.\n\nEach model contains PolynomialFeatures, StandardScaler and LinearRegression. Scaling addresses numerical feature magnitude; it does not remove the high-degree model’s ability to follow noise. Regularisation changes the fitting objective in the next parts. Keep the degree choice inside development rather than trying many degrees against a final test.',
  'import numpy as np\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfrom sklearn.pipeline import make_pipeline\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import PolynomialFeatures, StandardScaler\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression\nfrom sklearn.metrics import mean_absolute_error\nrng = np.random.default_rng(45)\nX = np.linspace(-1, 1, 18).reshape(-1, 1)\ny = np.sin(3 * X[:, 0]) + rng.normal(0, 0.16, len(X))\ngrid = np.linspace(-1, 1, 180).reshape(-1, 1)\ntruth = np.sin(3 * grid[:, 0])\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(6.5, 4))\nax.scatter(X[:, 0], y, color="#333333", label="Noisy training rows", s=22)\nax.plot(grid[:, 0], truth, linestyle="--", color="#888888", label="Simulator truth")\nfor degree in [1, 3, 12]:\n    m = make_pipeline(PolynomialFeatures(degree=degree, include_bias=False), StandardScaler(), LinearRegression()).fit(X, y)\n    p = m.predict(grid)\n    print("degree", degree, "train MAE", round(mean_absolute_error(y, m.predict(X)), 3), "validation MAE", round(mean_absolute_error(truth, p), 3))\n    ax.plot(grid[:, 0], p, label=f"Degree {degree}")\nax.set(xlabel="Teaching input", ylabel="Simulated response", title="Complexity is a development decision")\nax.legend(fontsize=8)\nfig.tight_layout()\nfig.savefig("chart.png", dpi=110)\nplt.close(fig)',
  'Show the growth in feature count for one input at degrees 1, 3 and 12.',
  'from sklearn.preprocessing import PolynomialFeatures\nfor degree in [1, 3, 12]:\n    p = PolynomialFeatures(degree=degree, include_bias=False).fit([[0.0], [1.0]])\n    print(degree, "represented columns:", p.n_output_features_)',
  'Write the correct next action when training error falls while validation error rises.',
  'observation = {"training error": "down", "validation error": "up"}\nprint(observation)\nprint("Investigate overfitting; keep the final holdout reserved")',
  [('Is the lowest training error enough to select a degree?', 'No; inspect development performance', 'Always', 'Only for a polynomial', 'A flexible representation can fit training noise.'),
   ('Does StandardScaler itself prevent high-degree overfitting?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only when include_bias=False', 'Scaling changes feature magnitude, not the model class’s flexibility.')],
  'Choose the highest degree because it passes close to every training dot.', 'Compare development errors and control complexity before final testing.',
  'The printed values come from each fitted model on the same training rows and the same separate simulator grid. The plot reveals interpolation behaviour that a training score alone can conceal.',
  figure=('Noisy training observations with degree-one, degree-three and degree-twelve polynomial fits', 'The dashed line is known simulator truth, not something available in a real project. Compare the printed development errors; this illustration does not use the final event-demand test.'))

L(2, '3. Regularisation trades fit for controlled coefficients',
  'Explain a coefficient penalty and why scaling belongs before penalised fitting.',
  [('Regularisation; penalty; alpha', 'A penalty discourages large fitted coefficients in addition to residual cost. alpha controls its strength; larger is not automatically better.'),
   ('Ridge; L2 penalty; np.linalg.norm', 'Ridge adds alpha times squared coefficient size to the training squared-error sum. np.linalg.norm computes the Euclidean coefficient size, the square root of the sum of squared coefficients. The next part develops the full Ridge pipeline.'),
   ('Feature scale and penalty', 'A coefficient’s numerical size depends on feature units. Comparable scaling is important when a penalty acts on coefficient magnitudes.')],
  'Ordinary least squares focuses on residual fit. Regularised regression adds a cost for coefficient size, so the fitted solution balances matching the training values against using large coefficients. This can stabilise a flexible representation, especially when features are related or there are many terms.\n\nThe penalty is applied to coefficients in the supplied representation. Changing hours to minutes changes the coefficient size needed for the same prediction, so a fixed penalty no longer has the same effect. Fit scaling within the training boundary before penalised regression. With polynomial features, scale the expanded columns that actually receive the penalty.\n\nRegularisation can increase training error and still improve unseen error. Too much can also underfit. Its strength must be selected using development evidence. A penalty does not repair target leakage, establish causal meaning, or guarantee the right representation. The example shows the coefficient norm shrinking as Ridge strength increases on one fixed standardised training table.',
  'import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import Ridge\nrng = np.random.default_rng(45)\nX = rng.normal(size=(60, 3))\ny = 4 * X[:, 0] + 2 * X[:, 1] + rng.normal(0, 0.3, 60)\nZ = StandardScaler().fit_transform(X)\nfor alpha in [0.1, 10.0, 1000.0]:\n    m = Ridge(alpha=alpha).fit(Z, y)\n    print("alpha", alpha, "coefficient norm", round(float(np.linalg.norm(m.coef_)), 3))',
  'Show that changing a feature unit changes the coefficient needed for the same equation.',
  'hours, cups_per_hour = 2.0, 30.0\nminutes, cups_per_minute = hours * 60, cups_per_hour / 60\nprint("Hours prediction:", hours * cups_per_hour)\nprint("Minutes prediction:", minutes * cups_per_minute)\nprint("Different numerical coefficients:", cups_per_hour, cups_per_minute)',
  'Record a pipeline order for penalised polynomial regression.',
  'steps = ["impute on train", "expand polynomial terms", "scale expanded train columns", "fit penalised regression"]\nfor number, step in enumerate(steps, 1):\n    print(number, step)',
  [('Does stronger regularisation always improve held-out error?', 'No; too much can underfit', 'Always', 'Only when the training score rises', 'Penalty strength trades flexibility against fit and needs development evaluation.'),
   ('Why scale before penalising coefficients?', 'Coefficient magnitude depends on feature units', 'Scaling creates target labels', 'It makes every coefficient causal', 'The penalty sees the numerical representation, not physical meaning.')],
  'Treat the largest alpha as automatically safest and best.', 'Choose a justified strength inside development and inspect both fit and generalisation.',
  'Every alpha sees the same standardised training table. The printed norm describes coefficient shrinkage, not a measured improvement in future prediction.',
  syntax='Pipeline([("poly", PolynomialFeatures(include_bias=False)),\n          ("scale", StandardScaler()), ("model", Ridge(alpha=1.0))])')

L(2, '4. Ridge: shrink a curved model without dropping terms',
  'Fit a scaled Ridge pipeline and interpret its L2 penalty.',
  [('Ridge; L2 penalty', 'Ridge minimises squared residual sum plus alpha times the squared coefficient norm. With ordinary settings the intercept is not penalised. Shrinkage does not generally set coefficients exactly to zero.'),
   ('regression_pipeline', 'The supplied lab helper composes median imputation, optional degree-two expansion, optional scaling and a final regressor. Inspect its source before using it as a shortcut.')],
  'Ridge is useful when a flexible numeric representation has large or unstable coefficients. Its squared penalty discourages large weights while retaining the supplied terms. This differs from selecting a smaller feature set. A coefficient can become small without disappearing exactly.\n\nThe example uses a separate fictional development dataset and compares three predeclared strengths on the same validation rows. It does not inspect the final project holdout. Polynomial expansion happens before scaling, so the penalty applies to the expanded standardised terms rather than raw guest-count powers.\n\nA single validation comparison is still a development choice with sampling variation. Day6 uses shared cross-validation folds for the final candidate comparison. Do not tune dozens of strengths on the final test and report the best one as untouched evidence. The meaning of alpha also depends on the estimator’s objective convention; matching a Ridge alpha numerically to a Lasso alpha is not a fair strength equivalence.',
  DEMAND_DEV + 'from sklearn.linear_model import Ridge\nfrom sklearn.metrics import mean_absolute_error\nfrom lab import regression_pipeline\nfor alpha in [0.1, 1.0, 100.0]:\n    m = regression_pipeline(Ridge(alpha=alpha), polynomial=True).fit(Xt, yt)\n    print("alpha", alpha, "validation MAE", round(mean_absolute_error(yv, m.predict(Xv)), 3))',
  'Inspect the expanded feature count and fitted Ridge coefficient count.',
  DEMAND_DEV + 'from sklearn.linear_model import Ridge\nfrom lab import regression_pipeline\nm = regression_pipeline(Ridge(alpha=1.0), polynomial=True).fit(Xt, yt)\nprint("Expanded columns:", m.named_steps["poly"].n_output_features_)\nprint("Fitted coefficients:", len(m.named_steps["model"].coef_))',
  'Verify that the fitted imputation medians came only from this development training subset.',
  DEMAND_DEV + 'import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import Ridge\nfrom lab import regression_pipeline\nm = regression_pipeline(Ridge(alpha=1.0), polynomial=True).fit(Xt, yt)\nprint("Training-only medians:", bool(np.allclose(m.named_steps["fill"].statistics_, Xt.median())))',
  [('Does Ridge generally remove features by setting weights exactly to zero?', 'No; it usually shrinks rather than sparsifies', 'Always', 'Only with positive alpha', 'The L2 penalty is not the L1 sparsity mechanism.'),
   ('Can equal numeric alpha values be assumed equivalent across Ridge and Lasso?', 'No; their penalty and objective conventions differ', 'Always', 'Only on the same dataset', 'A shared parameter name does not imply an identical optimisation tradeoff.')],
  'Interpret every tiny Ridge coefficient as a removed or irrelevant real-world feature.', 'Describe shrinkage in the fitted representation and evaluate the whole pipeline.',
  'The same validation rows and metric are used for all strengths. Each fit relearns preparation on Xt only. These are development results, not final-test rankings.')

L(2, '5. Lasso: sparsity is useful but not proof of irrelevance',
  'Explain an L1 penalty and inspect zero coefficients in a controlled example.',
  [('Lasso; L1 penalty', 'Lasso adds alpha times the sum of absolute coefficients to squared residual cost scaled by1/(2n). It can set some coefficients exactly to zero.'),
   ('Convergence; max_iter; tol', 'Lasso uses an iterative solver. max_iter limits its iterations and tol controls its stopping tolerance. A convergence warning requires investigation rather than suppression.')],
  'Lasso can produce a sparse fitted representation, which is useful when many supplied columns add little predictive value. Sparsity is a property of the chosen data, representation, penalty and fit; it does not prove a removed variable has no causal or domain relevance. Correlated features can substitute for one another.\n\nThe controlled example gives three independent random inputs, but the target primarily depends on the first. After scaling, Lasso with a declared strength may set the weak coefficients to zero. Read the executed result rather than assuming a certain number of zeros must occur for every sample.\n\nInspect solver convergence and validate the penalty strength inside development. Increasing max_iter blindly is not a complete diagnosis for poor scaling or an inappropriate model. This example uses a small, well-scaled problem with explicit iteration and tolerance settings and treats warnings as authoring failures. It does not tune against the final demand holdout.',
  'import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.pipeline import make_pipeline\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import Lasso\nrng = np.random.default_rng(45)\nX = rng.normal(size=(100, 3))\ny = 4 * X[:, 0] + rng.normal(0, 0.1, 100)\nm = make_pipeline(StandardScaler(), Lasso(alpha=0.2, max_iter=10000, tol=1e-7)).fit(X, y)\ncoef = m.named_steps["lasso"].coef_\nprint("Coefficients:", coef.round(3).tolist())\nprint("Zero coefficients:", int(np.count_nonzero(coef == 0)))',
  'Fit a scaled Lasso with a declared alpha and print the solver iteration count.',
  'import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.pipeline import make_pipeline\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import Lasso\nX = np.array([[1, 0], [2, 1], [3, 0], [4, 1]], dtype=float)\ny = [3.0, 5.0, 7.0, 9.0]\nm = make_pipeline(StandardScaler(), Lasso(alpha=0.1, max_iter=10000, tol=1e-7)).fit(X, y)\nprint("Iterations:", int(m.named_steps["lasso"].n_iter_))',
  'State what a zero coefficient does and does not establish.',
  'claims = {"unused in this fitted linear equation": True, "proven no causal relevance": False}\nfor claim, supported in claims.items():\n    print(supported, claim)',
  [('What can an L1 penalty produce?', 'Exactly zero fitted coefficients', 'Guaranteed causal feature selection', 'Perfect generalisation', 'Sparsity is a fitting property, not a causal certificate.'),
   ('Should a convergence warning be hidden to keep output clean?', 'No; investigate scaling, settings and solver behaviour', 'Always', 'Only on a small dataset', 'A warning signals an unresolved fitting issue.')],
  'A zero Lasso weight proves the original variable never matters in the real world.', 'Interpret sparsity within this representation and evaluate its stability and usefulness.',
  'Only the first random input drives the constructed target strongly. The coefficient vector shows the model’s regularised fit for this sample and alpha; it is not an external scientific conclusion.')

L(2, '6. Select complexity inside development and freeze it',
  'Keep degree and penalty choices separate from the final holdout.',
  [('Development selection; search budget', 'Trying representations or strengths consumes development evidence. Declare candidate settings and resist expanding the search because a final test result is disappointing.'),
   ('Refit after selection', 'Once the method is chosen with development evidence, fit that declared method on the allowed full training portion before final evaluation.')],
  'A degree and alpha are choices, not learned truths. Make a small declared comparison using validation or training-only cross-validation. Keep each preparation step inside the fitted pipeline. Record the candidates, score definition and chosen result so another reader can reproduce the decision.\n\nThe example compares three polynomial Ridge strengths using the separate fictional development table. It chooses the lowest validation MAE with a deterministic alpha tie-break. This demonstrates the mechanics of selection, not a recommendation that these strengths are universally best. The final project uses a different predeclared four-model comparison on training-only folds.\n\nAfter selection, do not reinterpret the same validation score as a fresh final test. Refit the chosen procedure on the allowed training data and evaluate a separately reserved final partition once. If the test prompts new feature work, that is a new development cycle needing fresh evaluation evidence. Reproducing a known test is useful for learning but is not independent confirmation.',
  DEMAND_DEV + 'from sklearn.linear_model import Ridge\nfrom sklearn.metrics import mean_absolute_error\nfrom lab import regression_pipeline\nresults = []\nfor alpha in [0.1, 1.0, 10.0]:\n    m = regression_pipeline(Ridge(alpha=alpha), polynomial=True).fit(Xt, yt)\n    results.append((mean_absolute_error(yv, m.predict(Xv)), alpha))\nmae, alpha = min(results)\nprint("Selected development alpha:", alpha)\nprint("Validation MAE:", round(mae, 3))\nprint("Not a final test result")',
  'Write the declared selection rule including its tie-break.',
  'plan = {"candidates": [0.1, 1.0, 10.0], "primary": "validation MAE", "tie_break": "smaller alpha", "final_test": "unseen"}\nprint(plan)',
  'Choose the lowest error deterministically from a supplied development table.',
  'results = [(12.0, 1.0), (11.0, 10.0), (11.0, 0.1)]\nerror, alpha = min(results)\nprint("Chosen alpha:", alpha, "development error:", error)',
  [('Can the validation score used to select alpha also be described as a fresh final test?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only if alpha is small', 'Selection has already used that evaluation feedback.'),
   ('What should follow development selection?', 'Freeze the procedure and evaluate a separately reserved holdout', 'Keep changing until the final test is perfect', 'Delete difficult rows', 'Final reporting should follow rather than guide the selection.')],
  'Choose alpha from final test performance and call the chosen result untouched.', 'Choose inside development, record the rule and preserve a final reporting boundary.',
  'The tuple ordering first minimises validation MAE and then the alpha for exact ties. The printed winner belongs to this declared development comparison only.')

L(3, '1. Engineer only information available at prediction time',
  'Audit a proposed feature set before making derived columns.',
  [('Feature engineering; domain knowledge', 'Feature engineering represents available inputs in ways an estimator can use. Domain knowledge guides useful candidates but does not excuse leakage.'),
   ('Deterministic row-wise transform', 'A fixed formula applied independently to each row does not learn dataset statistics. A mean, target encoding or selected threshold learned from records must fit inside the training boundary.')],
  'Useful features can express a relationship that a simple model cannot discover from its raw columns alone. But the first filter is availability, not predictive correlation. For event demand, planned guests and duration exist before service. Final cups sold do not. A future outcome disguised as a derived feature is still leakage.\n\nA fixed multiplication such as guests times planned hours can be applied row by row. A replacement median, selected target correlation or category target mean is different: it learns from records and must respect the fit boundary. Keeping transforms inside the pipeline makes their use consistent and easier to audit.\n\nThe feature audit below is an ordinary Python planning exercise. It does not measure predictive usefulness. Later we compare representations using the same development folds and estimator, so any reported improvement is tied to measured evidence. More features can hurt by adding noise, redundancy or excessive flexibility. A plausible story is a hypothesis, not a validation result.',
  'proposals = {"guests": "known", "planned_hours": "known", "forecast_c": "known", "guests_times_hours": "known", "final_cups_per_guest": "future outcome"}\nfor name, status in proposals.items():\n    print(name, "allowed:", status == "known")',
  'Compute a guests-times-hours interaction using only planned inputs.',
  'rows = [{"guests": 40, "hours": 2}, {"guests": 60, "hours": 3}]\nprint([r["guests"] * r["hours"] for r in rows])',
  'Separate fixed row formulas from fitted preparation statistics.',
  'operations = {"guests * hours": "fixed row transform", "training median": "fitted statistic", "target correlation selection": "fitted supervised choice"}\nfor operation, boundary in operations.items():\n    print(operation + ": " + boundary)',
  [('Does a future-outcome feature become safe because it is a ratio?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only after standardisation', 'A transformation does not change when its source information becomes available.'),
   ('Is a median calculated from all rows a fixed row-wise transform?', 'No; it learns from the dataset', 'Yes', 'Only with odd row counts', 'Its value depends on which records were included.')],
  'Add the highest-correlated columns before asking when their information exists.', 'Audit availability and fit boundaries, then evaluate useful representations.',
  'The derived interaction uses only two known planned fields. The final-cups ratio includes the target outcome and is excluded regardless of its likely predictive strength.')

L(3, '2. Interactions let one input change another input’s effect',
  'Explain and construct a guests-times-duration feature.',
  [('Interaction term', 'A product such as guests*hours lets the model’s predicted change per guest depend on duration. It does not itself establish a causal mechanism.'),
   ('interaction_only', 'PolynomialFeatures(interaction_only=True) includes products of distinct inputs but omits repeated powers such as guests².')],
  'An additive equation gives the same change per guest at every duration. If long events tend to use more cups per guest, a product feature can represent that relationship: b0+b1×guests+b2×hours+b3×guests×hours. The predicted per-guest change now depends on hours.\n\nThis is still linear in the fitted coefficients. It is more flexible in the input relationship, so it needs evaluation rather than automatic acceptance. In the simulator, the author deliberately included an interaction to create a controlled teaching opportunity. That known design is not evidence that the same interaction improves a real café model.\n\nThe example constructs the product by hand and with PolynomialFeatures. Consistent column meaning matters: the same planned-hour unit must be used during training and inference. If hours are converted to minutes, the product and its coefficient scale change. A well-contained pipeline preserves the declared representation, but cannot recognise a hidden unit mistake.',
  'import pandas as pd\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import PolynomialFeatures\nX = pd.DataFrame({"guests": [40.0, 60.0], "hours": [2.0, 3.0]})\nprint("Manual interaction:", (X.guests * X.hours).tolist())\np = PolynomialFeatures(degree=2, include_bias=False, interaction_only=True)\nvalues = p.fit_transform(X)\nprint("Names:", p.get_feature_names_out().tolist())\nprint("Rows:", values.tolist())',
  'For a constructed equation 10+guests+2*hours+0.1*guests*hours, compare adding one guest at two and four hours.',
  'def estimate(guests, hours):\n    return 10 + guests + 2 * hours + 0.1 * guests * hours\nfor hours in [2, 4]:\n    print(hours, "hours: per-guest model change", round(estimate(41, hours) - estimate(40, hours), 3))',
  'List degree-two interaction-only features for three inputs.',
  'from sklearn.preprocessing import PolynomialFeatures\np = PolynomialFeatures(degree=2, include_bias=False, interaction_only=True).fit([[1, 2, 3]])\nprint(p.get_feature_names_out(["guests", "hours", "forecast"]).tolist())',
  [('What can a guests*hours term represent?', 'A per-guest predicted change that depends on duration', 'A guaranteed causal effect', 'A new independently observed target', 'The product changes the mathematical relationship the model can express.'),
   ('Does interaction_only=True include guests²?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only after fit', 'It restricts products to distinct input features.')],
  'Describe a fitted interaction as proof that changing one input causes the target change.', 'Interpret the equation and verify predictive usefulness inside development.',
  'The product is 80 and 180 guest-hours for the two rows. The named expansion confirms the same representation without manually relying on column positions.')

L(3, '3. Curved features and controlled dimensional growth',
  'Represent a nonlinear numeric relationship while tracking the added columns.',
  [('Squared feature; feature count', 'A squared input can express curvature. Polynomial expansion adds many columns, so track its dimensionality and evaluate the resulting complexity.'),
   ('Fixed reference versus learned centre', 'Subtracting a domain-defined constant is a fixed formula. Choosing a centre from data is a fitted decision and must use the proper training boundary.')],
  'The simulator includes a curved temperature term to make a feature-engineering lesson possible. A straight additive temperature coefficient cannot fully represent that shape. A squared temperature feature can. Subtracting a fixed reference such as18°C changes the algebraic representation, but with an intercept and linear term it spans the same quadratic family.\n\nThe example constructs a single transparent feature rather than blindly generating thousands of powers. In real data, choose candidates from domain reasoning and evaluate them fairly. A convenient reference temperature is a teaching design choice, not a discovered universal optimum.\n\nExpanding every original input can introduce redundant terms. A binary promotion column squared equals itself; this adds no new information. Ridge can help control a redundant representation, but a more deliberate feature set may be easier to explain. Do not interpret raw expanded coefficients as independent effects, particularly when their units and correlations differ.',
  'import pandas as pd\nX = pd.DataFrame({"forecast_c": [8.0, 18.0, 28.0], "promotion": [0, 1, 1]})\nX["temperature_curve"] = (X.forecast_c - 18.0) ** 2\nprint(X.to_string(index=False))\nprint("Binary square adds information:", not (X.promotion ** 2).equals(X.promotion))',
  'Fit a quadratic representation to a constructed symmetric temperature-demand table.',
  'from sklearn.pipeline import make_pipeline\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import PolynomialFeatures\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression\nX, y = [[8.0], [18.0], [28.0]], [120.0, 20.0, 120.0]\nm = make_pipeline(PolynomialFeatures(degree=2, include_bias=False), LinearRegression()).fit(X, y)\nprint("At13C:", round(float(m.predict([[13.0]])[0]), 3))',
  'Count the expanded columns for the final four-input degree-two representation.',
  'from sklearn.preprocessing import PolynomialFeatures\np = PolynomialFeatures(degree=2, include_bias=False).fit([[100, 2, 18, 1]])\nprint("Original inputs:", p.n_features_in_)\nprint("Expanded columns:", p.n_output_features_)',
  [('Does squaring a0/1 feature create new information?', 'No; it equals the original feature', 'Always', 'Only after standardisation', 'The values remain0 and1, so the column is redundant.'),
   ('Does a plausible curved feature guarantee lower unseen error?', 'No; evaluate it', 'Always', 'Only for temperature', 'A representation hypothesis needs development evidence.')],
  'Add all powers and claim every extra column is new independent evidence.', 'Track representation size, redundancy and measured development usefulness.',
  'The fixed18-degree reference produces equal curved values at8 and28. The example distinguishes a chosen representation from an observed physical demand law.')

L(3, '4. Encode a category without a fake distance or target leak',
  'Build mixed-input preparation and keep category vocabulary inside training.',
  [('Nominal versus ordinal category', 'A nominal category has no inherent numerical ordering. Integer coding can invent distances that a linear or distance model interprets incorrectly.'),
   ('ColumnTransformer in regression', 'The same reviewed column branches from Week44 work with a regressor. Fitted numeric statistics and categorical vocabulary must remain inside the fold pipeline.')],
  'Suppose a later project includes venue type such as hall or garden. Coding hall=0 and garden=1 may be manageable for two categories, but arbitrary numbers for many types invent a ranked numerical relationship. One-hot encoding expresses separate categories without assuming equal steps between them.\n\nCombine a numeric pipeline with a categorical encoder using ColumnTransformer. Explicit column lists keep row ids and target fields outside the model. In a cross-validation comparison, the entire preparation-and-regression pipeline must be fitted separately inside each training fold. Fitting the category vocabulary or imputation on all rows first crosses that boundary.\n\nThis tiny table is a representation drill; it is not sufficient evidence for a venue-demand model. The final project intentionally has only four numeric inputs, so it does not add unnecessary category branches. Do not introduce a category target mean calculated from all labels and call it harmless encoding: that is a supervised fitted statistic needing additional care.',
  'import pandas as pd\nfrom sklearn.compose import ColumnTransformer\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import OneHotEncoder, StandardScaler\nfrom sklearn.pipeline import Pipeline\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import Ridge\nX = pd.DataFrame({"guests": [20, 40, 60, 80], "venue": ["hall", "garden", "hall", "garden"]})\ny = [30.0, 55.0, 75.0, 100.0]\nprep = ColumnTransformer([("number", StandardScaler(), ["guests"]), ("category", OneHotEncoder(handle_unknown="ignore", sparse_output=False), ["venue"])])\nm = Pipeline([("prepare", prep), ("model", Ridge(alpha=1.0))]).fit(X, y)\nprint("Prepared names:", m.named_steps["prepare"].get_feature_names_out().tolist())\nprint("Training prediction count:", len(m.predict(X)))',
  'One-hot encode three venue types and list the learned columns.',
  'from sklearn.preprocessing import OneHotEncoder\ne = OneHotEncoder(sparse_output=False, handle_unknown="ignore").fit([["hall"], ["garden"], ["cafe"]])\nprint(e.get_feature_names_out(["venue"]).tolist())',
  'Identify which proposed categorical transformation uses target labels.',
  'operations = {"one-hot training vocabulary": False, "mean demand per venue from target labels": True}\nfor operation, supervised in operations.items():\n    print(operation, "uses target:", supervised)',
  [('Does a category’s arbitrary integer code supply a meaningful distance?', 'Not generally', 'Always', 'Only if it fits in a DataFrame', 'A numerical storage type does not create an ordered domain meaning.'),
   ('Should mean target encoding use held-out labels?', 'No', 'Yes because it is called preparation', 'Only for a rare category', 'Target-derived statistics can leak answers directly.')],
  'Encode a category with all-data target means before cross-validation.', 'Keep reviewed fitted preparation inside the fold and treat supervised encoding carefully.',
  'The output names expose one scaled numeric feature and the learned venue indicators. The small training prediction count only checks representation mechanics, not generalisation.')

L(3, '5. Transform a skewed target and score in original units',
  'Explain a log-target model and its interpretation limits.',
  [('TransformedTargetRegressor; func; inverse_func', 'A wrapper transforms y during fit and inversely transforms predictions. It can keep log-target training and original-unit inference together.'),
   ('log1p; expm1; retransformation', 'log1p(y)=log(1+y) is defined here for nonnegative demand; expm1 reverses it. Inverting a predicted log value does not automatically recover the original conditional mean.')],
  'Some numerical targets have a wide positive range. A log transform can change the fitting emphasis from large absolute amounts toward relative scale, but it also changes the learning problem. Do not assume a log model is better simply because its log-space error number is smaller.\n\nTransformedTargetRegressor keeps the forward and inverse operations together. Its predict returns original-unit estimates, which you should compare against the original targets using the declared metric. Directly comparing log-space RMSE to cup-space RMSE is meaningless because their units differ.\n\nThere is also a statistical subtlety: exponentiating a predicted average log value is not generally the same as predicting the original-scale average. A real application may need an appropriate bias correction or a different objective. The exact toy relationship here avoids that noise issue so you can learn the interface. The final project does not silently introduce a log-target model or change its selection criterion.',
  'import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.compose import TransformedTargetRegressor\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression\nfrom sklearn.metrics import mean_absolute_error\nX = np.array([[0.0], [1.0], [2.0], [3.0]])\ny = np.expm1(1 + 0.5 * X[:, 0])\nm = TransformedTargetRegressor(regressor=LinearRegression(), func=np.log1p, inverse_func=np.expm1).fit(X, y)\np = m.predict(X)\nprint("Original-unit predictions:", p.round(3).tolist())\nprint("Original-unit training MAE:", round(mean_absolute_error(y, p), 6))',
  'Verify a log1p/expm1 round trip for nonnegative counts.',
  'import numpy as np\ny = np.array([0.0, 10.0, 100.0])\nprint("Round trip:", np.expm1(np.log1p(y)).round(6).tolist())',
  'Show that exponentiating an average log differs from an arithmetic mean.',
  'import numpy as np\ny = np.array([1.0, 9.0])\nprint("Arithmetic mean:", float(y.mean()))\nprint("Inverse average log:", round(float(np.expm1(np.log1p(y).mean())), 3))',
  [('Which unit should final MAE use for a cup-demand prediction task?', 'Original cups', 'Log values without disclosure', 'Any unit giving a smaller number', 'Evaluate the stated target and explain any transformed fitting objective.'),
   ('Does inverse-transforming a mean log prediction always give the original mean?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only when using expm1', 'Nonlinear inversion and averaging do not generally commute.')],
  'Compare a log-space metric directly with an original-cup metric and call the smaller number better.', 'Transform predictions back and evaluate a declared original-unit criterion.',
  'The wrapper learns the constructed linear relation in log1p space and returns original-scale predictions. The zero-like training error belongs to this exact toy formula, not real demand accuracy.')

L(3, '6. Measure a feature-only improvement with the same folds',
  'Compare two representations while keeping estimator, rows, folds and metric fixed.',
  [('KFold; cross_val_score; make_folds', 'KFold divides row positions into training/validation folds. cross_val_score fits a fresh clone on each training fold and evaluates its held-out fold. The supplied make_folds helper returns the same five position pairs on the project training table for every candidate.'),
   ('neg_mean_absolute_error; ablation', 'scikit-learn scorers use larger-is-better conventions, so MAE is returned negatively. Negate once for positive error. An ablation changes one declared component to inspect its contribution.')],
  'To claim a feature representation helped, isolate the change. Compare an additive linear pipeline with a degree-two linear pipeline using the same training rows, the same five folds, the same metric and the same final estimator. Here both final estimators are LinearRegression; only the optional expansion differs.\n\nEach pipeline fits imputation and scaling inside its own training fold. No final test label or all-data preparation is used. The simulated target deliberately contains curved and interaction relationships, so this is a controlled demonstration, not a promise that polynomials improve every dataset. Read the executed scores and their difference.\n\nCross-validation scores have variability, and trying many representations against the same folds can overfit development feedback. A measured improvement here supports this declared simulation comparison only. The final project later compares complete candidate pipelines and reserves a separate reporting holdout. Do not confuse that broader candidate comparison with a feature-only causal attribution.',
  'from lab import make_splits, make_folds, regression_pipeline\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import cross_val_score\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nd = make_splits()\nfolds = make_folds(d["X_train"])\nerrors = []\nfor label, expanded in [("Additive", False), ("Degree two", True)]:\n    m = regression_pipeline(LinearRegression(), polynomial=expanded)\n    values = -cross_val_score(m, d["X_train"], d["y_train"], cv=folds, scoring="neg_mean_absolute_error", error_score="raise")\n    errors.append(float(values.mean()))\n    print(label, "mean CV MAE", round(float(values.mean()), 3), "cups")\nprint("Measured MAE reduction:", round(errors[0] - errors[1], 3), "cups")\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(6, 3.7))\nax.bar(["Additive", "Degree two"], errors, color=["#376ea6", "#3d927a"])\nax.set(ylabel="Mean training-only CV MAE (cups)", title="Feature ablation: same linear estimator and folds")\nfig.tight_layout()\nfig.savefig("chart.png", dpi=110)\nplt.close(fig)',
  'Create five repeatable training-only folds and check one fold has disjoint fit/validation positions.',
  'from lab import make_splits, make_folds\nd = make_splits()\nfit, valid = make_folds(d["X_train"])[0]\nprint("Fit/validation rows:", len(fit), len(valid))\nprint("Overlap:", len(set(fit) & set(valid)))',
  'Convert negative MAE scorer values to positive errors exactly once.',
  'import numpy as np\nscorer_values = np.array([-12.0, -14.0, -13.0])\nerrors = -scorer_values\nprint("Errors:", errors.tolist())\nprint("Mean MAE:", float(errors.mean()))',
  [('Why keep the final estimator identical for this feature ablation?', 'To isolate the representation change', 'To guarantee perfect results', 'To remove the need for validation', 'Changing both algorithm and features would mix two explanations.'),
   ('What does a more negative neg_mean_absolute_error score mean?', 'Larger positive error', 'Better prediction automatically', 'More correct classifications', 'The scorer is minus MAE, so its sign must be interpreted consistently.')],
  'Change model, split and metric together, then attribute the improvement only to a new feature.', 'Keep comparison conditions fixed and identify the single changed component.',
  'Both bars are means across identical folds using only the reserved training portion. The printed reduction is measured for the author’s simulator and does not establish real customer performance.',
  figure=('Mean five-fold MAE for additive and degree-two representations with the same linear estimator', 'Smaller is better, in cups. The only changed model component is the polynomial expansion; each fold fits its own preparation and the final test is not used.'))
